# extra steps inserted before the closing check of each model answer (index = question order)
MORE = {
1: [{"svg": __import__("figmod").bank_svg("alias"), "text": "<b>Sketch.</b> The figure shows a triangular spectrum of half-width f_max = 700 Hz sampled at f_s = 1000 Hz: the copy centred at 1000 Hz reaches down to 300 Hz, so the band from 300 Hz to 700 Hz is covered twice and the sum (dashed) is no longer the original triangle."},
    "<b>Where the alias lands.</b> A line at f inside the overlap, say 700 Hz, has an image at f_s − f = 300 Hz from the copy at f_s. The baseband filter (cutoff f_s/2 = 500 Hz) passes that image, so the output contains a 300 Hz line that was never in x(t), and loses the true 700 Hz line.",
    "<b>Effect on amplitude.</b> A single aliased cosine keeps its amplitude. When a real component and an aliased one land on the same frequency they add with their phases, so the measured amplitude there can grow or shrink: the original spectrum cannot be read back from the sum."],
2: ["<b>Why uniform is reasonable.</b> When the signal changes by many steps between samples, the position of x inside its step is equally likely anywhere, so e_q is equally likely anywhere in ±Δ/2. A sine that spans the whole range at a frequency unrelated to f_s behaves this way closely.",
    "<b>Why the noise is called white.</b> Under the same conditions successive errors are nearly uncorrelated, so their power Δ²/12 is spread evenly from 0 to f_s/2. This is what makes oversampling useful: only the part of the noise inside the signal band matters.",
    "<b>Mid-tread note.</b> A mid-tread quantizer has a level at zero, so a zero input gives zero error; its noise power is the same Δ²/12 for a busy signal because the error is still spread over one step."],
3: ["<b>Why both conditions are needed.</b> Without time invariance the response to δ[n − k] would be a different sequence h_k[n] for each k, and the output would be Σ x[k]h_k[n], which is not a convolution. Without linearity the response to the sum would not be the sum of responses.",
    "<b>Reading the sum.</b> For a fixed n, the term x[k]h[n − k] is the input at time k weighted by how strongly the system still responds n − k samples later. For a causal system h[n − k] = 0 when k > n, so the sum runs only over the present and past inputs, k ≤ n.",
    "<b>Graphical evaluation.</b> Flip h[k] to h[−k], shift it by n to get h[n − k], multiply sample by sample with x[k] and add. For y[1] in the example below, h[1 − k] has the value 2 at k = 0 and 1 at k = 1, so y[1] = 2·2 + 1·1 = 5."],
4: ["<b>Spectrum picture.</b> On the positive axis the sampled spectrum has lines at 1000 Hz and at 500 Hz (from 4000 − 3500) inside 0 to 2 kHz, and further lines at 3000 Hz (4000 − 1000), 4500 Hz (8000 − 3500), 5000 Hz (4000 + 1000) and 7500 Hz (4000 + 3500).",
    "<b>Amplitude of the alias.</b> A single cosine keeps its amplitude when it folds, so the false 500 Hz tone has amplitude 2 V, the same as the 3500 Hz component it came from; its phase is reversed in sign, which for a cosine with zero phase makes no difference.",
    "<b>Energy check.</b> The power of x(t) is 3²/2 + 2²/2 = 4.5 + 2 = 6.5 W (into 1 Ω), and the power of the reconstructed signal is the same 6.5 W: aliasing moves power to the wrong frequency rather than losing it, which is why it is so hard to spot."],
5: ["<b>Strict versus usual rule.</b> The strict rule would put the stopband edge at f_s/2 = 4 kHz. Components between 4 and 5.4 kHz fold to between 2.6 and 4 kHz, above the highest wanted component, so a digital low-pass filter after the sampler can remove them; that is why the usual rule uses 5.4 kHz and needs a lower order.",
    "<b>Order with the strict rule.</b> With f_stop = 4 kHz and f_c = 3 kHz: N ≥ 4.000/(2 log₁₀ 1.333) = 4.000/(2 × 0.1249) = 16.0, so N = 17: twice the order, for no gain in the wanted band."],
6: ["<b>Graphical view.</b> Flip x[k] (or h[k]) and slide it. While 0 ≤ n ≤ 4 the flipped five-sample block only partly overlaps h[k], and the overlap grows by one sample each step; from n = 4 on it covers five samples of h, which shrink by the factor a each step.",
    "<b>Alternative route.</b> The input is u[n] − u[n − 5], so by linearity and time invariance y[n] = s[n] − s[n − 5], where s[n] = (1 − a^{n+1})/(1 − a) u[n] is the step response. For n ≥ 5 this gives (1 − a^{n+1} − 1 + a^{n−4})/(1 − a) = a^{n−4}(1 − a⁵)/(1 − a), the same as above."],
7: ["<b>Characteristic root versus pole.</b> In Z-transform language H(z) = 1/(1 − 0.5z⁻¹), with a pole at z = 0.5; the root λ = 0.5 of the characteristic equation is the same number, so both routes give the same stability verdict.",
    "<b>Transient and steady state.</b> The output splits into the steady part 2 (the forced response) and the transient −(0.5)ⁿ (the natural response). The transient halves every sample: after 7 samples it is 0.0078, under 0.4% of the final value.",
    "<b>A non-zero start.</b> If instead y[−1] = 2, then y[0] = 1 + 0.5 × 2 = 2, so C = 0 and y[n] = 2 for all n ≥ 0: the system starts already at its final value and shows no transient."],
8: ["<b>Overload.</b> The model holds only while −5 V ≤ x ≤ +5 V. A 6 V peak is clipped to the top level, 5 − Δ/2 = 4.98 V, an error of about 1.02 V, far beyond Δ/2; such errors are not described by Δ²/12.",
    "<b>Smaller signals.</b> A sine of amplitude 1 V (14 dB below full scale) gives SQNR = 49.92 − 20 log₁₀ 5 = 49.92 − 13.98 = 35.94 dB: the noise stays the same while the signal falls.",
    "<b>Mid-rise levels.</b> The output levels are −5 + (k + ½)Δ for k = 0 to 255, from −4.98 V to +4.98 V; an input of 1.000 V lies in step k = 153 (from 0.977 V to 1.016 V), whose level is 0.996 V, an error of −0.004 V."],
9: ["<b>Why the zone test works.</b> Sampling folds the axis at every multiple of f_s/2. A band inside one zone is mapped one to one onto 0 to f_s/2; a band that crosses a zone edge would be folded onto itself and overlap.",
    "<b>Margin at 18 kHz.</b> The band edges sit 2 kHz above the zone's lower edge (20 − 18) and 3 kHz below its upper edge (27 − 24), so the anti-aliasing band-pass filter has 2 to 3 kHz of room on each side."],
10: ["<b>Phase of the hold.</b> The delay τ/2 = 62.5 μs adds a phase lag of πfτ radians: 0.425π = 76.5° at 3.4 kHz. Because it is linear in f it is a pure time delay and does not change the waveform shape.",
     "<b>Natural sampling compared.</b> If the pulses followed the signal (natural sampling) instead of holding a value, the baseband copy would only be scaled by τ/T_s, with no droop; the aperture effect is a property of flat-top sampling and of the hold.",
     "<b>Where the equalizer goes.</b> In a digital system the inverse-sinc response is easiest to build as a short FIR filter before the DAC; in an analog system it is a peaking stage in the smoothing filter."],
11: ["<b>Why 4 kHz is the likely answer.</b> Of the candidates, only rates above 2 × 300 = 600 Hz can show a 300 Hz tone at all, and a system meant to carry signals up to about 1.5–2 kHz would use a rate near 4 kHz; the lower rates would alias much of the wanted band too.",
     "<b>Check of another candidate.</b> At f_s = 3.4 kHz: |3700 − 3400| = 300 Hz as well. Either rate is a valid answer if it is stated with this check."],
12: ["<b>Marginal case.</b> If the root moved to exactly z = 1, the mode would be a constant C_1: the impulse response would not decay, Σ|h[n]| would diverge, and a step input would make the output grow like n. So the boundary itself is already not BIBO stable.",
     "<b>Negative root behaviour.</b> The (−0.6)ⁿ term flips sign every sample, so h[n] ripples: h[2] = 0.52 is larger than h[1] = 0.2. This ripple comes from the negative pole, not from instability."],
13: ["<b>Where noise enters and leaves.</b> Quantization noise is added after the sampler, so the anti-aliasing filter does not reduce it; the discrete-time filter and the reconstruction filter do, by removing noise outside the signal band. Oversampling spreads the same Δ²/12 over a wider band, so a digital filter can remove more of it: doubling f_s lowers the in-band noise by about 3 dB.",
     "<b>Stability across the chain.</b> The analog filters and the hold are stable by construction (passive or with poles in the left half-plane). The only block that can become unstable is a recursive discrete-time filter, and only when a pole lies on or outside the unit circle; rounding its coefficients to the word length can move a pole near the circle, so poles are kept well inside it.",
     "<b>Aliasing in the output side.</b> The output side has the mirror problem: the images at kf_s ± f must be removed by the reconstruction filter, or they are heard as high-pitched tones. The same guard band that eases the anti-aliasing filter eases the reconstruction filter."],
}
